            "label": "T2: Taking a gap year before university",
            "text": "#Q Discuss what choice in your opinion is better for young people in preparing them for the future career.\n\n#A Nowadays, taking a gap year before university has become increasingly popular. While some people think that a gap year gives young people time to explore themselves and gain life skills, others argue that going to university immediately after school is a much better option. This essay will discuss both views and give my own opinion.\n\n#A On the one hand, there are several reasons why some people support taking a gap year. One main reason is that it gives young people time to discover what they really want to do. This is because many school leavers are not sure about their future careers when they are only eighteen. For example, a student who works as a volunteer for a few months may realise that he is interested in teaching or social work. In addition, another reason is that a gap year helps young people develop useful life skills. As a result, they become more independent and more mature before they start university.\n\n#A On the other hand, I believe that going to university immediately is a better choice. One main reason is that students can keep their study habits and motivation. For instance, after a long break, many young people find it hard to return to studying, and some even give up their plan to go to university. This may lead to fewer chances of getting a good job in the future. Another reason is that students can start their careers earlier. Therefore, they can gain work experience and become financially independent sooner than those who take a year off.\n\n#A In conclusion, although a gap year can help young people explore themselves and gain life skills, I believe that going to university immediately is a better way to prepare for their future careers."
