            "label": "T2: The shortage of qualified workers",
            "text": "#Q Discuss the extent to which you agree or disagree that vocational training should be encouraged to address the shortage of qualified workers.\n\n#A Nowadays, the shortage of qualified workers has become a matter of great concern to the public. While some people think that studying at university is the best way to get a good job, others argue that vocational training should be encouraged, especially for young people. I totally agree with the second view, and this essay will explain why.\n\n#A There are several reasons why vocational training should be encouraged. One main reason is that it can help solve the shortage of qualified workers. This is because vocational schools train students in practical skills that many companies really need. For example, in Vietnam, factories and hotels are always looking for skilled electricians, mechanics and chefs. In addition, another reason is that vocational training is shorter and cheaper than a university degree. As a result, young people from poor families can start working and earning money after only one or two years.\n\n#A Besides, encouraging vocational training also brings benefits to society. One main benefit is that it can reduce unemployment among university graduates, since fewer young people will compete for the same office jobs. For instance, every year, many graduates cannot find jobs that match their degrees, while many technical jobs are still available. This may lead to a waste of time and money for both families and the country. Another benefit is that skilled workers help the economy grow faster. Therefore, a country with more skilled workers can attract more foreign investment and develop its industries.\n\n#A In conclusion, I strongly believe that vocational training should be encouraged, because it can solve the shortage of qualified workers, save time and money for young people and bring benefits to society. Therefore, the government should invest more in vocational schools and change people's attitudes towards this kind of training."
